# D-03. A floating sine across a bridge rectifier into a load resistor. Whichever way the input pushes, two of the four
# diodes steer the current the same way through the load: the negative halves come out turned over. Every period of
# the input now gives two bumps, so the ripple is at twice the input frequency — and the peak is two diode drops short.
circuit:full-wave-rectifier("Full-wave bridge rectifier") {
    meta {
        summary: "A sine through a bridge rectifier: both halves come out positive, two diode drops lower, at twice the input frequency."
        description: """
            Idea: four diodes in a bridge send the current through the load the same way on both halves of the input.
            On one half one pair conducts, on the other the other pair; the negative halves come out turned over.

            Try: raise the amplitude: the bumps grow, two diode drops short of the input. Change the input frequency:
            the bumps come twice as often. Change the load: the shape stays, the current follows it.

            Watch: CH2, the input across the bridge, and CH1, the output: two bumps for every period of the input, no
            gaps between them but short flats at zero where the input is below two diode drops. The frequency of the
            output is twice that of the input.

            Ask: why is the peak two diode drops lower here, but only one in a half-wave rectifier? Why can the
            generator not have its own end on ground here?
            """
        category: diodes-and-rectifiers
        tags: diodes, example, d-03
        authors: "jMouse"
        license: "CERN-OHL-W-2.0"
    }
    parameter:A(12 V, in: 3 V .. 30 V, step: 0.5 V, "Input amplitude")
    parameter:F(50 Hz, in: 10 Hz .. 400 Hz, step: 10 Hz, "Input frequency")
    parameter:R_load(1 kΩ, in: 100 Ω .. 10 kΩ, step: 100 Ω, "Load resistance")
    part:G1(sine-generator, [positive: a, negative: b], amplitude: A, frequency: F)
    part:BR1(bridge-rectifier, [ac1: a, ac2: b, plus: out, minus: 0])
    part:R1(resistor, [out, 0], resistance: R_load)
    place:G1(at: 400 600, rotate: 90)
    place:BR1(at: 800 600)
    place:R1(at: 1200 600, rotate: 90)
    mark:Jm(junction, at: 800 840)
    mark:GND1(ground, at: 800 920)
    wire:W1(G1.positive, to: BR1.ac1, via: [400 400, 640 400, 640 600])
    wire:W2(G1.negative, to: BR1.ac2, via: [400 800, 960 800, 960 600])
    wire:W3(BR1.plus, to: R1.positive, via: [800 360, 1200 360])
    wire:W4(BR1.minus, to: Jm.j)
    wire:W5(R1.negative, to: Jm.j, via: 1200 840)
    wire:W6(Jm.j, to: GND1.ground)
    probe:CH1(out, 5 V/div)
    probe:CH2(a, against: b, 10 V/div)
    expect("the peak is two diode drops short of the input", from: "two conducting diodes in series: 12 V − 2 · VF ≈ 12 V − 2 · 0.75 V") {
        measure(maximum, out)
        within(10.2 V .. 10.8 V)
    }
    expect("the ripple comes at twice the input frequency", from: "full-wave: two bumps every period, 2 · 50 Hz") {
        measure(frequency, out, above: 5 V, after: 5 ms)
        near(100 Hz, +- 2%)
    }
    expect("and between the bumps it touches zero", from: "while |input| < 2 · VF no pair conducts") {
        measure(minimum, out)
        within(-0.01 V .. 0.01 V)
    }
    analysis(transient, for: 60 ms)
    view:transient_response(primary)
}
